A||||7||william_thorniii||85 GT (4-sp) surge at slow speeds||||9||
Z||000000||william_thorniii||02-14-2000||12:27 PM||||Friends,<P>Symptoms:  surges or j-e-r-k-s at constant slow speeds and when starting from a stop.  <P>Treatments of Symptoms:  Increase speed for the condition that occurs at constant slow speeds.  Slip the clutch when starting the vehicle from a stop. <P>Other Information:  I cannot detect much if any clutch.  In other words the revolutions seem to increase linearly with speed.  <P>Assumptions:  Not a driveline problem.<P>Does anyone see any possible culprets that could cause this problem?  Otherwise should I further examine the clutch or look to the powerplant?  <P>Any and all help is GREATLY appreciated,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||9
Z||000001||mwbackus||02-14-2000||05:13 PM||||If it only does this (eg. constant speed surging) after it has warmed up sufficiently, I would suggest checking the EGR system closely. A DIY diagnosis can be performed quite easily:<P>1) With the engine cold, check the EGR valve operation.  You should be able to operarate the valve by hand from underneath. It should move freely with very little effort.  An older EGR valve should be replace anyway, as the crud that builds up internally with time will eventually cause incomplete valve closure/seal which is nearly impossible to detect externally.  <P>2) Bypass the EGR solenoid temporarily by sealing (tape) the vacuum source closed(two plastic line double plug at the solenoid) and disconnecting the electrical harness.  Drive at operating temp. for at least 15-30 min. to determine if the problems has been rectified.  <P>3)  With the engine at operating temp., attach a vacuum gauge to the line which connects directly to the EGR valve, ensure  a good seal between the vacuum line and gauge.  Increase engine speed to approx. 2500 rpm and hold it there until you see considerable vacuum on the gauge.  Return to idle and observe the vacuum reading, it should drop to zero almost immediatly or the EGR solenoid is defective (eg. not relieving vacuum to the valve correctly).  <BR>Note: a wet finger can be substituted for the guage.. yuck!!<P>4)  Reconnect the EGR valve vacuum line, increase engine speed to approx. 3000 rpm., holding it there for several seconds, then abruptly disconnect the vacuum line and listen for the EGR valve to snap shut.  If there is no indication of this then the EGR valve diaphram is probably defective and causing a vacuum leak.<P>Mike<P><p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 02-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||02-14-2000||05:23 PM||||Mike,<P>Thanks for the post.  I will do some checking on the EGR.  I think that I have verified the fact that the EGR works by actuating it by hand, then again that could have been my last car.  I will also verify that this only happens when the car is warmed.  Usually I allow my car to warm for about a minute before heading out, but do not recall if it happens before operating temperature is reached.  I live on a sizable hill and would have to slip the clutch anyway.  <P>I have a question about your step #3... I have a vacuum pump with an integral gage.  I should be able to test it with this correct?  If so do you know what vacuum I should be able to pull before the EGR opens?  <P>Thanks though for the heads up.  I will also try to double check my EGR tube. <P>Many Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000003||mwbackus||02-14-2000||05:28 PM||||Gee.. you have a vacuum pump with an integral guage!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  That will work just fine, disregard the wet finger idea.  I have a table somewhere that lists the EGR vacuum vs. rpm.  Just have to fine it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrFeeArrow||02-14-2000||05:55 PM||||Check the throttle position sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula||02-14-2000||07:34 PM||||my v6 surged for a month before the ignition coild flew south. <P>I changed the wires,plugs distributor cap and rotor and the coil and plugged my egr vacuum tube and the problem wet away.<P>don't block the tube if you drive far distances, my fiero goes under a hundred miles a week.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||02-14-2000||07:56 PM||||Will the EGR test work the same for the Iron Duke?  My motor problems are different, but it was suggested a while ago that I might check the EGR valve.  Isn't there some way of rebuilding them?||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||02-14-2000||08:25 PM||||Formula,<P>I have already replaced all of those items less the coil as routine maintenance when I purchased this car.  Also I wouldn't imagine it to be the coil since this is a low load application.  I would think the coil to be a more likely culprit if I experienced problems at WOT.  Then again, I have never claimed to understand or to begin to understand electricity.  All I know is that it hurts like HELL when you catch 440 when trying to hook up an injection molding machine (Perhaps that answers a lot of questions out there about me)!<P>Monkey,<P>All EGR's work essentially the same to my knowledge.  A good test is to press up on the diaphram and see if engine speed changes.  Then you can go through the more technical tests.  The first EGR test that I would do if I were you would be to check the tube for cracks.  <P>Everyone,<P>Thanks for all of the responses!  So far I have gotten some leads that I wouldn't have ever thought of trying. <P>Keep 'em coming,  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
